[patterns/application/micro-frontend/PTN-APP-MFE-008-micro-frontend-integration-overview.md#problem-details]

### Problem Details

**Problem 1: Tight Coupling Through Direct Imports**

Teams frequently create build-time dependencies by importing code between modules:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Direct import creates tight coupling
import { ClaimSummary } from '@gainwell/enterprise-claims-mfe';

function ProviderDetailPage({ providerId }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <ProviderProfile providerId={providerId} />
      {/* Using Claims MFE component directly */}
      <ClaimSummary providerId={providerId} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Impact:**
- Provider MFE cannot deploy without coordinating with Claims MFE version
- Breaking changes in Claims MFE require coordinated updates in Provider MFE
- Defeats independent deployment—the core benefit of micro-frontends

**Real-World Example:** Provider MFE imports Eligibility MFE components to display member eligibility status on provider pages. When Eligibility team updates component props, Provider MFE breaks in production, requiring emergency coordination to deploy compatible versions.

**Problem 2: Unstructured Event-Based Communication**

Teams implement event communication without contracts or versioning:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Untyped events without versioning
eventBus.publish('claim-submitted', { id: 123, amt: 1500 });

// Another module subscribes, but event shape changes break integration
eventBus.subscribe('claim-submitted', (data) => {
  // Assumes data.amount exists, but publisher changed to data.amt
  processClaim(data.amount);  // undefined, breaks silently
});
```

**Impact:**
- No compile-time or runtime validation of event contracts
- Silent failures when event payload changes
- Difficult to trace which modules publish/subscribe to events
- Performance overhead when many modules subscribe to all events

**Real-World Example:** Claims MFE changes event payload structure from `{ claimId, amount }` to `{ claim: { id, totalAmount } }`. Care Coordination MFE, which subscribes to these events, breaks silently because it expects flat structure. Issue discovered days later when care gap analysis shows missing data.

**Problem 3: State Synchronization Complexity**

Multiple modules caching shared data create consistency problems:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Each module caches user independently
// Claims MFE
const [user, setUser] = useState(await fetchUser());

// Provider MFE
const [user, setUser] = useState(await fetchUser());

// User updates profile in Settings MFE
// Claims and Provider MFE now have stale data
```

**Impact:**
- Stale data displayed when shared state changes
- Duplicated API calls to fetch same data across modules
- Memory overhead from redundant caching
- Race conditions when multiple modules update shared state

**Real-World Example:** User updates their display name in Profile Settings. Claims and Provider MFEs continue showing old name for remainder of session because each module cached user independently. User confused why update didn't take effect.

**Problem 4: Workflow Coordination Overhead**

Complex workflows spanning modules require coordination logic:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Workflow logic scattered across modules
// Claims MFE: After claim approved, manually navigate to Payment MFE
function handleClaimApproval(claim) {
  await approveClaim(claim.id);
  // Hardcoded navigation to Payment MFE
  navigate(`/payments/new?claimId=${claim.id}&amount=${claim.amount}`);
}

// Payment MFE: Manually parse query params and fetch claim details again
function NewPaymentPage() {
  const { claimId } = useQueryParams();
  const claim = await fetchClaimDetails(claimId);  // Duplicate API call
  // ...
}
```

**Impact:**
- Workflow logic fragmented across modules makes changes difficult
- URL-based data passing is brittle and limited to simple data types
- Duplicated API calls when modules pass IDs and receivers refetch full objects
- No transaction guarantees across module boundaries

